            "Rough-Cut Capacity Planning evaluates capacity feasibility by translating aggregate product family demand forecasts into required operating hours across critical resource clusters. These resource clusters encompass internal manufacturing lines, specialized bottleneck machinery, skilled labor pools subject to collective bargaining constraints, regional warehouse storage capacities, and strategic tier-one raw material suppliers. The mathematical engine powering RCCP relies on Bills of Resources (BOR), alternatively referred to as product load profiles. A Bill of Resources establishes the standard run hours, setup times, labor hours, and specialized tooling requirements demanded by one aggregate unit of a given product family across each critical work center. By multiplying the monthly volumetric demand by the Bill of Resources matrix, supply planners generate the aggregate workload profile for every critical asset over the entire planning horizon.",
